<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Naxos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Naxos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Largest island of the Cyclades, an Archaic marble-working power whose failed siege by Persia in 499 BCE set off the Ionian Revolt.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Naxos was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, building on a long Cycladic and Mycenaean past. Its fertile valleys and white marble made it the leading island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyclades">Cyclades</a> by the 7th century BCE. Tradition linked it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Naxian sculptors made colossal kouroi, the Naxian Sphinx at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a> and the Lions on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delos">Delos</a>. The tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lygdamis-of-naxos">Lygdamis</a>, helped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a>, ruled in the mid-6th century BCE, when the great unfinished temple gateway, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portara">Portara</a>, was begun. In 499 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristagoras-of-miletus">Aristagoras of Miletus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> failed to capture Naxos, a fiasco that triggered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> sacked Naxos in 490 BCE. It was the first member to try to leave the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> and was subdued c. 470 BCE. Later it joined Hellenistic leagues and passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> by 146 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:41.79%;width:15.00%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Naxos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lygdamis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Dionysus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Classical Naxos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=naxos" aria-label="Naxos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5318 842 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Naxos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5638 1098L6140 1251M5638 1098L5591 1068M5638 1098L5591 1068M5638 1098L5632 1088"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5591 1068h0M5632 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5638 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5638 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-523&amp;focus=naxos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

